1. Fibre Channel provides a dedicated high speed networking technology which gives a storage network between servers and storage systems.

A basic FC environment looks like:

Linux Server
     |
     ↓
FC HBA
     |
     ↓
FC Switch
     |
     ↓
Storage Array
     |
     ↓
LUN

2. What is SAN?

SAN = Storage Area Network

A SAN is a dedicated network that allows servers to access shared storage.

For example:

              SAN
               |
       +-------+-------+
       |               |
       ↓               ↓
    Server 1         Server 2
       |               |
       +-------+-------+
               |
               ↓
          Storage Array

Fibre Channel is one of the common technologies used to build a SAN.

iSCSI can also be used to provide SAN-style block storage over Ethernet/IP.

3. Fibre Channel HBA

A Fibre Channel server normally uses an HBA (Host Bus Adapter).

An HBA is similar in concept to a network adapter, but it is designed for Fibre Channel storage communication.

Normal Network:

Server
  ↓
NIC
  ↓
Ethernet


Fibre Channel:

Server
  ↓
FC HBA
  ↓
Fibre Channel

4. WWPN and WWNN

Fibre Channel devices use unique identifiers called WWNs.

WWN = World Wide Name

Two terms you may encounter are:

WWPN

World Wide Port Name

Identifies an FC port.

WWNN

World Wide Node Name

Identifies an FC node/device.

Example:

FC HBA
   |
   +--- WWPN
   |
   +--- WWNN

WWPN is commonly used when configuring SAN connectivity.

5. Fibre Channel Switch

An FC switch connects servers and storage devices within the Fibre Channel network.

Example:

Server
  |
FC HBA
  |
  ↓
FC Switch
  |
  ↓
Storage

The FC switch is part of the SAN fabric.

6. What is Zoning?

Zoning is configured on the Fibre Channel switch.

It controls which FC devices are allowed to communicate with each other.

Also a zone can allow a particular server HBA to communicate with a particular storage port.

7. What is LUN Masking?

LUN masking is generally configured on the storage system.

It controls which hosts are allowed to access specific LUNs.

For example:

Storage Array
     |
     +--- LUN 10 → Server 1
     |
     +--- LUN 20 → Server 2

Server 1 should not automatically be able to access LUN 20.

8. What is Multipathing?

Multipathing provides multiple paths between a server and storage.

Without multipathing:

Server
   |
   |
   ↓
Storage

If the path fails, storage access can be lost.

With multipathing:

              Storage
              /     \
             /       \
         Path 1     Path 2
           |           |
           ↓           ↓
        Switch 1    Switch 2
           |           |
           +-----+-----+
                 |
               Server

If one path fails:

Path 1 → FAILED

Path 2 → WORKING

The server can continue accessing the storage through the remaining path.

Multipathing provides:

Redundancy
High availability
Better resilience against path failures

Linux commonly uses Device Mapper Multipath (DM-Multipath) for this purpose.
